The file is complete. All the information needed is present. Here is the precise structured report.

---

## 1. INTERNAL STATE — Member Field Inventory

All members are declared at lines 375-384:

```cpp
// line 375-384
KvFlashConfig cfg_;
std::vector<ggml_tensor *> attn_k_, attn_v_;
std::vector<ChunkState> chunks_;
std::vector<int> free_blocks_;
std::vector<uint8_t> zero_buf_;
KvFlashStats stats_;
size_t k_seg_bytes_ = 0, v_seg_bytes_ = 0, chunk_bytes_ = 0;
int n_blocks_ = 0, n_head_kv_ = 0, cur_chunk_ = 0;
uint64_t clock_ = 0;
uint64_t epoch_ = 0;
```

**`ChunkState` inner struct (lines 315-320):**
```cpp
struct ChunkState {
    int      block = -1;       // pool block index, -1 = not resident
    bool     on_host = false;  // backing store holds valid bytes
    uint64_t last_use = 0;
    std::vector<uint8_t> host_data;
};
```

Field-by-field inventory:

| Field | Type | Role |
|-------|------|------|
| `cfg_` | `KvFlashConfig` | chunk_tokens, pool_tokens, sink_chunks, tail_window_chunks |
| `attn_k_` | `vector<ggml_tensor*>` | Per-full-attention-layer K cache tensors (pool-sized), shape `[head_dim, pool_tokens, n_head_kv]` |
| `attn_v_` | `vector<ggml_tensor*>` | Same for V |
| `chunks_` | `vector<ChunkState>` | Per-logical-chunk state: block assignment, host residency, host bytes, LRU clock |
| `free_blocks_` | `vector<int>` | Pool block indices currently unoccupied (stack; back = next to allocate) |
| `zero_buf_` | `vector<uint8_t>` | Scratch zeros for zeroing freed blocks; max(k_seg_bytes_, v_seg_bytes_) bytes |
| `stats_` | `KvFlashStats` | Counters: page_outs, page_ins, host_bytes, moved_bytes |
| `k_seg_bytes_` | `size_t` | Bytes for one K head-segment: `chunk_tokens * K0->nb[1]` |
| `v_seg_bytes_` | `size_t` | Same for V: `chunk_tokens * V0->nb[1]` |
| `chunk_bytes_` | `size_t` | Total host_data size per chunk: `(k_seg_bytes_ + v_seg_bytes_) * n_head_kv_ * n_layers` |
| `n_blocks_` | `int` | `pool_tokens / chunk_tokens` — total physical blocks |
| `n_head_kv_` | `int` | KV head count (from `K0->ne[2]`) |
| `cur_chunk_` | `int` | Append-head chunk index — used to define the protected tail window |
| `clock_` | `uint64_t` | LRU tick; incremented on every `slot_for` hit |
| `epoch_` | `uint64_t` | Residency-change counter; callers use to invalidate cached masks |

**What serialize must capture:** `chunks_` (all block assignments + on_host flags + host_data payloads), `cur_chunk_`, and the resident KV bytes from the GPU pool tensors. `free_blocks_`, `clock_`, `epoch_`, `stats_` are reconstructable and need not be serialized (though clock/epoch should be reset on deserialize).

---

## 2. LOGICAL→PHYSICAL MAPPING

**`slot_for` (lines 165-191) — allocating lookup:**
```cpp
int slot_for(int64_t pos) {
    const int c = (int)(pos / cfg_.chunk_tokens);
    const int prev_cur_chunk = cur_chunk_;
    if (c > cur_chunk_) cur_chunk_ = c;
    if ((int)chunks_.size() <= c) chunks_.resize(c + 1);
    ChunkState & st = chunks_[c];
    if (st.block < 0) {
        if (!ensure_free_block()) {
            cur_chunk_ = prev_cur_chunk;
            return -1;
        }
        st.block = free_blocks_.back();
        free_blocks_.pop_back();
        epoch_++;
        if (st.on_host) {              // recall: restore paged-out bytes
            copy_chunk(c, st.block, /*to_host=*/false);
            stats_.page_ins++;
            stats_.moved_bytes += chunk_bytes_;
        }
    }
    st.last_use = ++clock_;
    return st.block * cfg_.chunk_tokens + (int)(pos % cfg_.chunk_tokens);
}
```

Physical slot formula: `block * chunk_tokens + (pos % chunk_tokens)`.

**`slot_of` (lines 241-245) — non-allocating const lookup:**
```cpp
int slot_of(int64_t pos) const {
    const int c = (int)(pos / cfg_.chunk_tokens);
    if (c >= (int)chunks_.size() || chunks_[c].block < 0) return -1;
    return chunks_[c].block * cfg_.chunk_tokens + (int)(pos % cfg_.chunk_tokens);
}
```

**`alloc_span` (lines 150-160):** Calls `slot_for` for every position in `[kv_start, kv_start+n_tok)` in order; fails immediately if any slot cannot be allocated.

**`page_out` (lines 195-210) — eviction to host:**
```cpp
bool page_out(int c) {
    if (c >= (int)chunks_.size() || chunks_[c].block < 0) return false;
    ChunkState & st = chunks_[c];
    if (!st.on_host) {
        st.host_data.resize(chunk_bytes_);
        stats_.host_bytes += (int64_t)chunk_bytes_;
    }
    copy_chunk(c, st.block, /*to_host=*/true);
    zero_block(st.block);
    st.on_host = true;
    free_blocks_.push_back(st.block);
    st.block = -1;
    epoch_++;
    stats_.page_outs++;
    stats_.moved_bytes += chunk_bytes_;
    return true;
}
```

After `page_out`: `st.block == -1`, `st.on_host == true`, `st.host_data` holds `chunk_bytes_` bytes (populated by `copy_chunk`), the physical block is zeroed and returned to `free_blocks_`.

**`page_in` (lines 214-217):** Simply delegates to `slot_for` for the first token of the chunk; `slot_for` detects `st.on_host` and calls `copy_chunk(..., to_host=false)` to restore from `host_data`.

---

## 3. `copy_chunk` AND SEGMENT WALK

**`copy_chunk` (lines 345-360):**
```cpp
void copy_chunk(int c, int block, bool to_host) {
    ChunkState & st = chunks_[c];
    uint8_t * p = st.host_data.data();
    for (size_t l = 0; l < attn_k_.size(); l++) {
        for (int kv = 0; kv < 2; kv++) {
            ggml_tensor * t = kv == 0 ? attn_k_[l] : attn_v_[l];
            const size_t seg = kv == 0 ? k_seg_bytes_ : v_seg_bytes_;
            for (int h = 0; h < n_head_kv_; h++) {
                const size_t off = (size_t)block * cfg_.chunk_tokens * t->nb[1] + (size_t)h * t->nb[2];
                if (to_host) ggml_backend_tensor_get(t, p, off, seg);
                else         ggml_backend_tensor_set(t, p, off, seg);
                p += seg;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Segment ordering (layer-major, K-before-V, head-minor):**
- Outer: `l` in `[0, n_layers)` — layer index
- Middle: `kv` in `{0=K, 1=V}` — K before V
- Inner: `h` in `[0, n_head_kv_)` — head index

**Physical offset formula** for head `h` of K/V tensor `t` at block `block`:
```
off = block * chunk_tokens * t->nb[1]   // row stride into the block
    + h * t->nb[2]                       // head stride
```
Segment size is `seg` bytes (`chunk_tokens` rows × row stride `t->nb[1]`).

**`for_each_segment` — the iteration to factor:**

The segment walk is the triple loop above. The helper should look like:

```cpp
// Proposed signature — does NOT exist yet, must be written from scratch
template<typename Fn>
void for_each_segment(int c, int block, Fn && fn) const {
    // fn(layer, kv, head, tensor, byte_offset, seg_bytes)
    for (size_t l = 0; l < attn_k_.size(); l++) {
        for (int kv = 0; kv < 2; kv++) {
            ggml_tensor * t = kv == 0 ? attn_k_[l] : attn_v_[l];
            const size_t seg = kv == 0 ? k_seg_bytes_ : v_seg_bytes_;
            for (int h = 0; h < n_head_kv_; h++) {
                const size_t off = (size_t)block * cfg_.chunk_tokens * t->nb[1]
                                 + (size_t)h * t->nb[2];
                fn(l, kv, h, t, off, seg);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**`for_each_segment` does NOT currently exist** — it must be written. `copy_chunk` and `zero_block` both inline the same triple loop; this helper would be the factored form. Both existing functions would collapse to a single `for_each_segment` call.

---

## 4. KV BYTE LAYOUT + BYTES-PER-SLOT

**Established in `attach` (lines 96-103):**
```cpp
n_head_kv_ = (int)K0->ne[2];
k_seg_bytes_ = (size_t)cfg.chunk_tokens * K0->nb[1];
v_seg_bytes_ = (size_t)cfg.chunk_tokens * attn_v[0]->nb[1];
chunk_bytes_ = (k_seg_bytes_ + v_seg_bytes_) * (size_t)n_head_kv_ * attn_k.size();
```

- `K0->nb[1]` is the **row stride in bytes** — for a `[head_dim, pool_tokens, n_head_kv]` tensor, `nb[1]` is the stride of the second dimension = bytes per token-row (= `head_dim * element_size` if contiguous).
- `k_seg_bytes_` = bytes for one K head across `chunk_tokens` rows = `chunk_tokens * nb[1]`.
- `chunk_bytes_` = total host_data size per chunk = `(k_seg_bytes_ + v_seg_bytes_) * n_head_kv_ * n_layers`.
- **Bytes per slot** (one token, one layer, one head, one K or V): `nb[1]` (= `head_dim * element_size`).
- **Bytes per chunk** across the whole model: `chunk_bytes_`.

The element type is whatever the quantization is (K0->type); the code is type-agnostic — it copies raw bytes via `ggml_backend_tensor_get/set`.

**host_data layout** (the serialized blob for one chunk) follows the `copy_chunk` loop order:
```
[layer_0_K_head_0][layer_0_K_head_1]...[layer_0_V_head_0]...[layer_0_V_head_{H-1}]
[layer_1_K_head_0]...
...
[layer_{L-1}_V_head_{H-1}]
```
Total: `n_layers * n_head_kv * (k_seg_bytes_ + v_seg_bytes_)` bytes = `chunk_bytes_`.

---

## 5. WHAT `deserialize` MUST REBUILD

**Reset first:**
```cpp
reset();  // clears chunks_, refills free_blocks_, sets cur_chunk_=0, advances epoch_
```

**Then for each logical chunk `c` in the serialized blob (logical order 0..N-1):**

1. Call `slot_for((int64_t)c * cfg_.chunk_tokens)` — this allocates a block (evicting if needed), sets `chunks_[c].block`, handles recall if somehow `on_host` (it won't be post-reset).
2. Write the KV bytes for this chunk into the assigned block using `copy_chunk`'s write path — i.e., `copy_chunk(c, chunks_[c].block, /*to_host=*/false)` — **after** populating `chunks_[c].host_data` with the blob bytes.

**The write primitive IS `copy_chunk` with `to_host=false`** — it calls `ggml_backend_tensor_set(t, p, off, seg)` for each segment. This primitive exists; it is private but accessible from within the class.

**There is no public write primitive.** `deserialize` must be a member function (or `friend`) to access `copy_chunk` directly. Alternatively, `for_each_segment` as a public template + direct `ggml_backend_tensor_set` calls could serve public callers.

**If the pool fills during deserialize** (more logical chunks than `n_blocks_`), `slot_for` will call `ensure_free_block()` → `page_out(victim)`, writing the victim's bytes back to `host_data`. After deserializing all chunks, overflowed chunks will be in `host_data` with `block==-1, on_host==true`. This is correct — the pager naturally handles it, and `reselect()` or subsequent `slot_for` calls will bring them back in on demand.

---

## 6. SINK/TAIL + `is_identity`

**`min_pool_tokens` (lines 75-77):**
```cpp
static int min_pool_tokens(const KvFlashConfig & cfg) {
    return (cfg.sink_chunks + cfg.tail_window_chunks + 2) * cfg.chunk_tokens;
}
```
The `+2` means: 1 evictable victim + 1 partially-filled append head always needed.

**`is_identity` (lines 227-233):**
```cpp
bool is_identity() const {
    for (int c = 0; c < (int)chunks_.size(); c++) {
        if (chunks_[c].block >= 0 && chunks_[c].block != c) return false;
        if (chunks_[c].block < 0 && chunks_[c].on_host) return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```
Holds from `reset()` until the first eviction of the current request. When `is_identity()` is true, chunk `c` is at block `c`, so the pool tensor bytes are in logical order — a simple full-tensor copy suffices instead of per-chunk gather. `deserialize` can exploit this: if loading into a fresh `reset()` state AND the number of chunks fits in the pool, `is_identity()` will be true after loading and the pool tensor is a flat block of logical KV in order.

**Protected-window invariant in `ensure_free_block` (lines 329-333):**
```cpp
if (c < cfg_.sink_chunks) continue;                              // sinks always resident
if (c > cur_chunk_ - 1 - cfg_.tail_window_chunks) continue;     // tail window always resident
```

`deserialize` does not need to reconstruct these explicitly — they fall out naturally from the fact that the pager never evicts protected chunks. As long as the logical order is restored correctly, the first `reselect()` call will enforce the policy.

---

## Summary of Primitives That Do NOT Exist Yet

| Primitive | Status |
|-----------|--------|
| `for_each_segment(c, block, fn)` | DOES NOT EXIST — must be written; factor from `copy_chunk` + `zero_block` |
| `serialize()` | DOES NOT EXIST |
| `deserialize()` | DOES NOT EXIST |
| Public write-into-slot from external byte buffer | DOES NOT EXIST — `copy_chunk(..., false)` is private; `deserialize` must be a member or `friend` |
| Read primitive for resident chunk to external buffer | DOES NOT EXIST as public API — `copy_chunk(..., true)` is private; `serialize` same constraint |

`ggml_backend_tensor_get` (read) and `ggml_backend_tensor_set` (write) are the GGML backend primitives that do the actual D2H/H2D transfer; both are already used in `copy_chunk`. The `for_each_segment` helper + member `serialize`/`deserialize` methods are the entire write surface needed.
